    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2305.15964_rag::chunk_001</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2311.06025_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2305.15964_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2305.09617_rag::chunk_002</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2212.13138_rag::chunk_013</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2309.11295_rag::chunk_002</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2212.13138_rag::chunk_004</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2309.11295_rag::chunk_007</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2212.13138_rag::chunk_001</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2305.15964_rag::chunk_004</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2203.15556_rag::chunk_016</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2212.13138_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2203.02155_rag::chunk_026</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2203.15556_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We propose a method called Clinical Prediction with Large Language Models (CPLLM). This method involves fine-tuning a LLM using prompts</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">By using a large language model, organizations, including healthcare systems, can quickly process large amounts of text data, de-identify medical records</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Clinical Prediction with Large Language Models (CPLLM). This method involves fine-tuning a LLM using prompts</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">LLMs such as GPT-4</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Large Language Models Encode Clinical Knowledge</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">general LLMs typically produce medical advice based solely on their encoded knowledge, without considering specific knowledge in the medical domain</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">auto-regressive LLMs are still susceptible to hallucinations and generate erroneous content</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Pathways Language Model (PaLM), a dense decoder-only Transformer model, has 540 billion parameters</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">LLMs have shown abili</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">resulted LLMs difficult to generalize</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">enabling us to fully exploit the reasoning capabilities of the LLM in recursively retrieving the most pertinent and valuable knowledge.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">models ability to generate factual, safe responses to questions that require nuanced answers, typical in real-world medical question-answering.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">CoT prompting can elicit reasoning abilities in suciently large language models</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We used two different LLMs, Llama2, which is a general LLM (Touvron et al., 2023b) and BioMedLM</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Several studies have shown that LLMs have the capacity to act as implicit knowledge bases</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">we fine-tuned two LLMs Llama2 (13B) and BioMedLM (2.7B)</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">LLMs are foundation models [ 10 ], large pre-trained AI systems that can be repurposed with minimal eort across numerous domains and diverse tasks.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The LLM initially generates a preliminary report based on the visual description</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The trend so far in large language model training has been to increase the model size</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">diverse benchmark to assess the clinical knowledge and question answering capabilities of LLMs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">LLMs can process text data at high speed, making them an efficient tool for de-identification tasks.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">highlighting the potential of LLMs in the medical domain</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">large language models should be deployed only with great care, or not at all. Examples include high-stakes domains such as medical diagnoses</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">large language models face several challenges, including their overwhelming computational requirements</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/S0/R1#patient_diagnosis_history">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
